1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,948
A hidden message in an image. A cipher
from ancient Rome. A phone number that played

2
00:00:04,948 --> 00:00:05,655
a recording.

3
00:00:06,555 --> 00:00:11,230
And behind all of it, an anonymous group
that wanted to hire the smartest

4
00:00:11,230 --> 00:00:12,639
codebreakers on Earth.

5
00:00:13,239 --> 00:00:15,382
Nobody has ever proved who they were.

6
00:00:16,282 --> 00:00:23,132
The last message they signed arrived in
April 2017. After that, nothing verified.

7
00:00:23,252 --> 00:00:27,403
So who built this? What did they want? And
why did they stop?

8
00:00:27,523 --> 00:00:32,579
The best explanation is coming. But first,
you have to climb the ladder the way the

9
00:00:32,579 --> 00:00:33,676
first solvers did.

10
00:00:33,796 --> 00:00:35,184
One rung at a time.

11
00:00:35,684 --> 00:00:38,877
Rung one. January 4, 2012.

12
00:00:38,997 --> 00:00:40,921
A post appears on 4chan.

13
00:00:41,041 --> 00:00:46,481
If you have never seen 4chan, here is what
matters. It is an anonymous image board.

14
00:00:46,601 --> 00:00:49,041
No registration. No names.

15
00:00:49,161 --> 00:00:54,546
There is no formal archive. Most posts
expire, or get deleted, within hours.

16
00:00:55,356 --> 00:00:57,523
It is a place where things vanish.

17
00:00:57,643 --> 00:01:00,117
So a puzzle posted there should have
vanished too.

18
00:01:00,237 --> 00:01:01,240
It did not.

19
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:05,160
The message was an image. Nothing special
about how it looked.

20
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:09,323
But one commenter suggested opening it in
a plain text editor.

21
00:01:09,443 --> 00:01:14,874
One early solver, scrolling that board
late on a Friday night in January, tried it.

22
00:01:14,994 --> 00:01:17,409
At the bottom of the file, there was text.

23
00:01:18,309 --> 00:01:22,986
TIBERIVS CLAVDIVS CAESAR says, and then a
string of nonsense characters.

24
00:01:23,586 --> 00:01:25,793
Nonsense, if you do not know Rome.

25
00:01:25,913 --> 00:01:29,067
Tiberius Claudius was the fourth Roman
emperor.

26
00:01:29,187 --> 00:01:29,970
Four.

27
00:01:30,090 --> 00:01:35,133
And a Caesar cipher swaps each letter for
another a fixed number of places along the

28
00:01:35,133 --> 00:01:35,673
alphabet.

29
00:01:35,793 --> 00:01:38,205
So shift the letters back four places.

30
00:01:38,955 --> 00:01:39,971
It worked.

31
00:01:40,091 --> 00:01:42,424
The nonsense turned into a web address.

32
00:01:42,544 --> 00:01:44,204
Rung one, cleared in minutes.

33
00:01:44,704 --> 00:01:46,438
Rung two. The duck.

34
00:01:46,558 --> 00:01:49,549
The address led to a picture of a plastic
duck.

35
00:01:49,669 --> 00:01:52,886
Under it, the words: "WOOPS just decoys
this way."

36
00:01:53,006 --> 00:01:56,187
And then: "Looks like you can't guess how
to get the message out."

37
00:01:56,307 --> 00:01:57,420
Odd phrasing.

38
00:01:57,540 --> 00:01:59,018
Guess. Out.

39
00:01:59,138 --> 00:02:03,247
Somebody in the thread noticed that those
two words might be a hint.

40
00:02:03,367 --> 00:02:08,557
There is a piece of software called
OutGuess. It extracts data hidden inside images.

41
00:02:08,677 --> 00:02:13,598
That is steganography. Hiding a message
inside something that looks harmless.

42
00:02:13,718 --> 00:02:15,249
They ran the duck through it.

43
00:02:16,089 --> 00:02:18,042
It gave up a link to a subreddit.

44
00:02:18,162 --> 00:02:19,632
And on that page?

45
00:02:19,752 --> 00:02:21,508
A row of Mayan numerals.

46
00:02:21,628 --> 00:02:23,896
Several lines of garbled letters.

47
00:02:24,016 --> 00:02:27,007
Two images, labeled "welcome" and
"problems?"

48
00:02:27,507 --> 00:02:30,810
That is the point where the game stops
looking like a prank.

49
00:02:30,930 --> 00:02:32,769
Because pranksters are lazy.

50
00:02:32,889 --> 00:02:34,099
This was layered.

51
00:02:34,219 --> 00:02:39,106
Each step needed a different kind of
thinking. Ancient ciphers. Modern software.

52
00:02:39,106 --> 00:02:41,428
Number systems from another continent.

53
00:02:41,548 --> 00:02:43,770
And the solvers were not alone anymore.

54
00:02:43,890 --> 00:02:46,834
Someone posted a link to an anonymous chat
room.

55
00:02:46,954 --> 00:02:49,966
Dozens of strangers. Screen names only.

56
00:02:50,086 --> 00:02:55,105
Everyone chasing the same thread, in a
place where the original posts were already

57
00:02:55,105 --> 00:02:55,901
disappearing.

58
00:02:56,021 --> 00:03:00,764
The solver we have been following later
said this was the moment it started to feel

59
00:03:00,764 --> 00:03:01,507
a bit creepy.

60
00:03:01,627 --> 00:03:06,564
Because most trolls, they pointed out, do
not put this much energy into a joke.

61
00:03:07,464 --> 00:03:10,604
Here is the first thing you should know
about the theories.

62
00:03:10,724 --> 00:03:14,355
Some of the commenters that night
suspected something specific.

63
00:03:14,475 --> 00:03:17,094
They thought this was a recruiting
exercise.

64
00:03:17,214 --> 00:03:19,809
And they thought the recruiter might be
the NSA.

65
00:03:19,929 --> 00:03:22,658
That idea would follow this puzzle for
years.

66
00:03:22,778 --> 00:03:24,409
It would never be proved.

67
00:03:24,529 --> 00:03:25,732
Hold onto it.

68
00:03:26,232 --> 00:03:28,353
Rung three. The Holy Grail.

69
00:03:28,473 --> 00:03:31,630
Back at home, that same solver kept going
alone.

70
00:03:31,750 --> 00:03:33,594
Scribbling on index cards.

71
00:03:33,714 --> 00:03:38,762
The next layers involved book codes. King
Arthur. The quest for the Holy Grail.

72
00:03:38,882 --> 00:03:43,061
A book code, by the way, is a cipher that
points into a specific text.

73
00:03:43,181 --> 00:03:44,645
No book, no answer.

74
00:03:44,765 --> 00:03:46,692
So you have to work out which book.

75
00:03:46,812 --> 00:03:48,039
Hours went by.

76
00:03:48,159 --> 00:03:51,150
Then a message began to emerge from the
cards.

77
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:54,181
"Call us on us tele phone number..."

78
00:03:54,301 --> 00:03:55,552
And then digits.

79
00:03:56,152 --> 00:03:58,464
The solver blurted it into the chat room.

80
00:03:58,584 --> 00:04:00,211
"I'm getting a phone number."

81
00:04:00,331 --> 00:04:02,346
And the room did not believe them.

82
00:04:02,466 --> 00:04:04,595
The more advanced commenters doubted it.

83
00:04:04,715 --> 00:04:06,777
The less advanced insulted them.

84
00:04:06,897 --> 00:04:08,619
That is the internet for you.

85
00:04:08,739 --> 00:04:10,562
But a private message arrived.

86
00:04:10,682 --> 00:04:12,324
"You're way ahead of the others."

87
00:04:12,444 --> 00:04:14,930
An invitation to a smaller, private chat.

88
00:04:15,050 --> 00:04:17,111
So, with that, the solver dialed.

89
00:04:17,861 --> 00:04:19,316
A recording answered.

90
00:04:19,436 --> 00:04:22,120
Here is what it said, close to word for
word.

91
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:24,474
"Very good. You have done well."

92
00:04:24,594 --> 00:04:29,410
"There are three prime numbers associated
with the original final image."

93
00:04:29,530 --> 00:04:34,351
"3301 is one of them. You will have to
find the other two."

94
00:04:34,471 --> 00:04:39,693
"Multiply all three of these numbers
together and add a dot com on the end to find

95
00:04:39,693 --> 00:04:40,648
the next step."

96
00:04:40,768 --> 00:04:42,662
"Good luck. Good-bye."

97
00:04:43,262 --> 00:04:45,207
And that is where the number comes from.

98
00:04:45,327 --> 00:04:47,282
3301.

99
00:04:47,402 --> 00:04:48,398
A prime.

100
00:04:48,518 --> 00:04:50,467
The solver checked the first image.

101
00:04:50,587 --> 00:04:54,777
The pixel dimensions were 509 by 503.

102
00:04:55,527 --> 00:04:56,732
Both prime.

103
00:04:56,852 --> 00:05:01,191
Two primes, hidden in plain sight, in the
shape of the picture itself.

104
00:05:01,311 --> 00:05:07,162
Multiply them with 3301, add dot com, and
you get an address.

105
00:05:07,282 --> 00:05:09,271
An image of a cicada appeared.

106
00:05:09,391 --> 00:05:11,953
Above it, a countdown. Three days.

107
00:05:12,073 --> 00:05:17,017
And hidden inside the cicada, run through
OutGuess once more, another message.

108
00:05:17,137 --> 00:05:19,251
"You have done well to come this far."

109
00:05:19,371 --> 00:05:20,942
"Patience is a virtue."

110
00:05:21,062 --> 00:05:27,623
"Check back at 17:00 on Monday, 9 January
2012, UTC."

111
00:05:28,323 --> 00:05:30,098
It was around two in the morning.

112
00:05:30,218 --> 00:05:32,329
The solver had reached the next level.

113
00:05:32,449 --> 00:05:37,737
And what had begun as an online lark was
about to leave the screen and enter the

114
00:05:37,737 --> 00:05:38,464
real world.

115
00:05:39,264 --> 00:05:42,635
That is where one magazine's account of
the story breaks off.

116
00:05:42,755 --> 00:05:45,224
So let me tell you what else is on record.

117
00:05:45,344 --> 00:05:47,743
Because the puzzle did not stay online.

118
00:05:47,863 --> 00:05:50,596
The clues ran across several kinds of
medium.

119
00:05:50,716 --> 00:05:53,499
Digital images with hidden information.

120
00:05:53,619 --> 00:05:55,103
A telephone message.

121
00:05:55,223 --> 00:05:56,216
Websites.

122
00:05:57,056 --> 00:06:00,048
And paper signs, with QR codes on them.

123
00:06:00,168 --> 00:06:05,569
They were found on streets in Paris, in
Warsaw, in Miami, and in a suburb of Sydney.

124
00:06:05,689 --> 00:06:08,196
Physical things. Out in the world.

125
00:06:08,316 --> 00:06:11,510
Solvers met cryptography. Steganography.

126
00:06:11,630 --> 00:06:15,031
References to literature, poetry, art and
music.

127
00:06:15,151 --> 00:06:19,664
Among the works the puzzle used were a
poem called Agrippa, a poem from the

128
00:06:19,664 --> 00:06:22,673
Mabinogion, and a book called The Book of
the Law.

129
00:06:22,793 --> 00:06:25,159
And every clue carried the same signature.

130
00:06:25,279 --> 00:06:27,837
The same OpenPGP private key.

131
00:06:27,957 --> 00:06:32,459
That is a cryptographic way of proving a
message came from the same author.

132
00:06:32,579 --> 00:06:34,395
Anyone can copy a logo.

133
00:06:35,145 --> 00:06:37,007
Nobody can fake that key.

134
00:06:37,127 --> 00:06:41,449
It is the one thing that let solvers tell
the real thing from imitations.

135
00:06:41,569 --> 00:06:43,899
Remember that. It matters later.

136
00:06:44,499 --> 00:06:46,995
The first puzzle ran for nearly a month.

137
00:06:47,115 --> 00:06:48,258
Then it ended.

138
00:06:48,378 --> 00:06:53,878
And one year later, on January 4, 2013, a
second round began.

139
00:06:53,998 --> 00:06:57,262
Same day. Same date. Like clockwork.

140
00:06:57,382 --> 00:07:03,117
And on January 4, 2014, a third round
appeared. This time on Twitter.

141
00:07:03,867 --> 00:07:06,198
The third puzzle remains unsolved.

142
00:07:06,318 --> 00:07:09,193
Not "unsolved for a while." Unsolved.

143
00:07:09,693 --> 00:07:11,694
But was the second round solved?

144
00:07:11,814 --> 00:07:12,578
Yes.

145
00:07:12,698 --> 00:07:18,750
One of the people who solved the first
puzzle of 2013 later described what came

146
00:07:18,750 --> 00:07:19,133
next.

147
00:07:19,253 --> 00:07:21,556
Those who solved it were asked questions.

148
00:07:21,676 --> 00:07:24,249
About their support for information
freedom.

149
00:07:24,369 --> 00:07:26,094
About online privacy.

150
00:07:26,214 --> 00:07:28,880
About freedom, and rejecting censorship.

151
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,329
Answer well, and you got an invitation.

152
00:07:31,449 --> 00:07:32,903
To a private forum.

153
00:07:33,023 --> 00:07:35,437
And once in, you were given an assignment.

154
00:07:35,557 --> 00:07:41,200
Devise a project. Finish it. A project
meant to further the ideals of the group.

155
00:07:41,950 --> 00:07:43,819
That solver did not finish.

156
00:07:43,939 --> 00:07:47,121
They had been working on a method of
general decryption.

157
00:07:47,241 --> 00:07:48,983
And the website was removed.

158
00:07:49,103 --> 00:07:50,247
Just like that.

159
00:07:50,367 --> 00:07:54,151
A second winner documented the experience
in a video series.

160
00:07:54,271 --> 00:07:59,570
The description calls it a "more
realistic, fact based view of the organization."

161
00:07:59,690 --> 00:08:01,866
Realistic. Fact based.

162
00:08:01,986 --> 00:08:06,016
That tells you something about what the
rest of the internet was saying.

163
00:08:06,136 --> 00:08:13,024
Today that winner helps run community
efforts around Cicada 3301 on a Discord

164
00:08:13,024 --> 00:08:13,650
server.

165
00:08:13,770 --> 00:08:18,249
Other communities of solvers still gather
on message boards and forums.

166
00:08:18,369 --> 00:08:19,427
Still looking.

167
00:08:20,127 --> 00:08:21,403
So who were they?

168
00:08:21,523 --> 00:08:23,201
Let's go through the theories.

169
00:08:23,321 --> 00:08:25,732
Theory one. A spy agency.

170
00:08:26,512 --> 00:08:30,976
The NSA. The CIA. MI6. Mossad.

171
00:08:31,096 --> 00:08:34,186
Every one of these has been floated as a
recruiter.

172
00:08:34,306 --> 00:08:36,349
The logic is easy to follow.

173
00:08:36,469 --> 00:08:42,273
Cryptography, security, anonymity. Exactly
the skills an intelligence agency wants.

174
00:08:42,393 --> 00:08:45,148
And remember those commenters on that
first night.

175
00:08:45,268 --> 00:08:48,007
Theory two. A cyber mercenary group.

176
00:08:48,127 --> 00:08:50,296
A private outfit hiring hackers.

177
00:08:50,416 --> 00:08:53,162
Theory three. A "Masonic conspiracy."

178
00:08:53,282 --> 00:08:56,408
That one is in the record, though I would
not bet on it.

179
00:08:56,528 --> 00:08:59,026
Theory four. A secret society.

180
00:08:59,146 --> 00:09:03,583
One with a goal of improving cryptography,
privacy and anonymity.

181
00:09:03,703 --> 00:09:06,751
Theory five. A cult. Or a religion.

182
00:09:06,871 --> 00:09:08,929
And theory six. A game.

183
00:09:09,049 --> 00:09:10,822
An alternate reality game.

184
00:09:10,942 --> 00:09:13,467
Except that there is a problem with that
one.

185
00:09:14,337 --> 00:09:18,250
No company or individual has tried to
monetize it.

186
00:09:18,370 --> 00:09:21,749
No product. No sponsor. No reveal.

187
00:09:21,869 --> 00:09:24,801
A game with no seller is an odd kind of
game.

188
00:09:25,401 --> 00:09:28,763
Now, what do the people who actually
solved the puzzle say?

189
00:09:28,883 --> 00:09:34,910
According to several who solved the 2012
round, the group normally recruits by other

190
00:09:34,910 --> 00:09:36,417
means. Not by puzzle.

191
00:09:36,537 --> 00:09:40,923
But they wanted people with cryptography
and computer security skills.

192
00:09:41,043 --> 00:09:43,168
So they built a puzzle to find them.

193
00:09:43,288 --> 00:09:44,943
Which is a clue in itself.

194
00:09:45,063 --> 00:09:46,503
A puzzle is a filter.

195
00:09:46,623 --> 00:09:49,239
Anyone who gets through it has proved
something.

196
00:09:49,359 --> 00:09:53,419
Whoever built it wanted to test the very
skills the puzzle demanded.

197
00:09:54,319 --> 00:09:57,677
But that tells us what they wanted. Not
who they were.

198
00:09:58,377 --> 00:09:59,889
There is one more twist.

199
00:10:00,009 --> 00:10:01,357
The accusations.

200
00:10:01,477 --> 00:10:08,808
In 2012, police in a province of Chile,
the Investigative Police, claimed that

201
00:10:08,808 --> 00:10:11,721
Cicada 3301 was a hacker group.

202
00:10:11,841 --> 00:10:14,713
They said it engaged in illegal
activities.

203
00:10:14,833 --> 00:10:16,349
And Cicada answered.

204
00:10:16,469 --> 00:10:17,916
With a signed statement.

205
00:10:18,036 --> 00:10:21,015
Denying any involvement in illegal
activity.

206
00:10:21,135 --> 00:10:23,184
Then, July 2015.

207
00:10:23,304 --> 00:10:29,494
A group calling itself "3301" hacked into
Planned Parenthood's database.

208
00:10:29,614 --> 00:10:32,164
Same number. Same name, near enough.

209
00:10:32,284 --> 00:10:34,359
For a moment, the two looked like one.

210
00:10:35,139 --> 00:10:38,044
But Cicada issued another signed
statement.

211
00:10:38,164 --> 00:10:41,250
They were "not associated with this group
in any way."

212
00:10:41,370 --> 00:10:45,783
And they did not "condone their use of our
name, number, or symbolism."

213
00:10:45,903 --> 00:10:49,297
Later, the hacker group confirmed it.
There was no connection.

214
00:10:49,417 --> 00:10:50,959
Notice what happened there.

215
00:10:51,079 --> 00:10:53,870
The real Cicada could be told from the
fake.

216
00:10:53,990 --> 00:10:55,176
By the signature.

217
00:10:55,876 --> 00:10:57,124
Then it went quiet.

218
00:10:57,244 --> 00:11:01,951
A new clue appeared on Twitter on January
5, 2016.

219
00:11:02,071 --> 00:11:05,549
One day after the date everyone had
learned to expect.

220
00:11:05,669 --> 00:11:10,783
And then, in April 2017, the last
verified, signed message.

221
00:11:11,623 --> 00:11:14,656
It denied the validity of any unsigned
puzzle.

222
00:11:14,776 --> 00:11:15,951
Read that again.

223
00:11:16,071 --> 00:11:21,477
It means that anything posted after,
anything claiming to be a new round, without

224
00:11:21,477 --> 00:11:22,945
that key, is not them.

225
00:11:23,065 --> 00:11:26,034
And no verified signed puzzle has appeared
since.

226
00:11:26,634 --> 00:11:30,348
Those are the facts. Now, what is the best
explanation?

227
00:11:30,468 --> 00:11:31,437
Honestly?

228
00:11:31,557 --> 00:11:34,121
The evidence supports a narrow conclusion.

229
00:11:34,241 --> 00:11:36,478
Somebody built a recruitment filter.

230
00:11:36,598 --> 00:11:41,057
It was hard enough to need serious skill
in cryptography and security.

231
00:11:41,177 --> 00:11:45,096
It asked about privacy, freedom of
information, and censorship.

232
00:11:45,216 --> 00:11:49,447
It then put winners to work on projects
meant to further those ideals.

233
00:11:49,567 --> 00:11:53,052
That is consistent with a group that cared
about privacy.

234
00:11:53,922 --> 00:11:57,276
But it is also consistent with almost
everything else.

235
00:11:57,396 --> 00:11:59,193
An agency could build that.

236
00:11:59,313 --> 00:12:00,602
A society could.

237
00:12:00,722 --> 00:12:02,603
A group of enthusiasts could.

238
00:12:02,723 --> 00:12:04,492
There is no confirmed answer.

239
00:12:04,612 --> 00:12:06,318
And the weak points are real.

240
00:12:06,438 --> 00:12:10,537
The agency theory has no confirmation from
any agency.

241
00:12:10,657 --> 00:12:13,528
The mercenary theory has no known payout.

242
00:12:13,648 --> 00:12:15,981
The game theory has no known seller.

243
00:12:16,101 --> 00:12:20,697
The society theory has no known members
willing to say so on the record.

244
00:12:21,507 --> 00:12:23,966
Every theory is missing the same piece.

245
00:12:24,086 --> 00:12:24,887
Proof.

246
00:12:25,587 --> 00:12:27,239
And the legacy is strange.

247
00:12:27,359 --> 00:12:33,221
In 2014, the United States Navy released a
cryptographic challenge based on these

248
00:12:33,221 --> 00:12:34,669
recruitment puzzles.

249
00:12:34,789 --> 00:12:37,176
It was called Project Architeuthis.

250
00:12:37,296 --> 00:12:43,670
In 2015, a Person of Interest episode
called "Nautilus" featured a large worldwide

251
00:12:43,670 --> 00:12:46,002
game of cryptographic puzzles.

252
00:12:46,122 --> 00:12:48,529
A nautilus shell instead of a cicada.

253
00:12:48,649 --> 00:12:54,561
The creator said on record that Cicada
3301 was the inspiration.

254
00:12:54,681 --> 00:12:58,306
He called it, quote, "a very interesting
concept out there."

255
00:12:58,426 --> 00:13:06,499
And in 2021 came a comedy thriller, Dark
Web: Cicada 3301, directed by Alan

256
00:13:06,499 --> 00:13:07,468
Ritchson.

257
00:13:07,588 --> 00:13:12,763
The Washington Post once listed it among
the top five eeriest unsolved mysteries of

258
00:13:12,763 --> 00:13:13,573
the internet.

259
00:13:13,693 --> 00:13:18,431
Another description called it the most
elaborate and mysterious puzzle of the

260
00:13:18,431 --> 00:13:19,231
internet age.

261
00:13:19,351 --> 00:13:21,777
Yet at the center there is a plastic duck.

262
00:13:21,897 --> 00:13:23,219
A Roman emperor.

263
00:13:23,339 --> 00:13:24,372
And a number.

264
00:13:25,122 --> 00:13:26,380
A prime number.

265
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:28,455
3301.

266
00:13:29,255 --> 00:13:30,759
Think about the first night.

267
00:13:30,879 --> 00:13:36,296
A few dozen strangers, in a place built to
forget everything, remembering one thing

268
00:13:36,296 --> 00:13:36,883
together.

269
00:13:37,003 --> 00:13:39,530
And one of them said: it's a phone number.

270
00:13:39,650 --> 00:13:41,371
And nobody believed them.

271
00:13:41,491 --> 00:13:42,484
They were right.

272
00:13:43,084 --> 00:13:46,238
If this kind of story keeps you up at
night, subscribe.

273
00:13:46,358 --> 00:13:48,077
There are more tabs to open.

